      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)={(x+\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The parabola is written in vertex form, \n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the leading coefficient and $(h,k)$ is the vertex. \n\n- Since $h=-\\frac{5}{6}$ and $k=-\\frac{1}{2},$ the vertex is $(-\\frac{5}{6},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since $a=1>0,$ the parabola opens upwards.\n\nBecause the parabola opens upwards, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/fe58a2679e6075434e7042c3b2741a6fba1aea7b0042c8e1145aae6798588f96.png)",
