    "q-90776": {
      "dom_id": "step-q90776",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+5?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=5.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-4)}{2\\cdot 2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(1)+5 \\\\  & =2-4+5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,3),$ and the range of the function is $[3,\\infty ).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/a8fc15df2b24054485bb07330f40919ebf13cb9969073a733b9426a28dab20b5.png)",
