      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=-5(x-1)(x-2)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-5<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=2,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+2}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5(x-1)(x-2) \\\\  & =-5(\\frac{3}{2}-1)(\\frac{3}{2}-2) \\\\  & =-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{4}),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/426a8277921ca2c8282567eeb076c3dcf1f0f1c729c4ebd2e5d624f51b78d61e.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
