    "q-250644": {
      "dom_id": "step-q250644",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x-8).$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=8,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{2+8}{2}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=5$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-2)(x-8) \\\\  & =(5-2)(5-8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(5,-9),$ and the range of the function is $[-9,\\infty ),$ i.e. $-9\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/84c22aee72ebd52e3dab84a72ad1d402813c3a38794c653cd4cb0e31bfef3836.png)",
