    "q-250649": {
      "dom_id": "step-q250649",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-4(x-1)(x-4).$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)\\le $\n{{field-3}}",
      "worked_solution": "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+4}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4(x-1)(x-4) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{5}{2}-1)(\\frac{5}{2}-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{5}{2},9),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,9],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 9.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14244652/assets/70dc863f5f089c9434d783495c31b77fd7a0ac84a852d2b32222b445f463efe3.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
